FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** Jonathan Hartley wrote most of the Python code that survives today, and he is the only active author who holds a large share of it. If he stops answering, the handover loses its main source of knowledge. The two other large contributors have not committed in years, so they are already out of reach for maintenance, even if they still answer emails.

The Python figures below come from surviving-lines run with `--include '*.py'` in the repository. The whole-repository figures come from the ownership brief computed at commit 475b3d4. The interpretation is mine.

| Author | Non-blank lines | With blank lines | Share | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Jonathan Hartley | 843 | 1,065 | 52.8% | 2026-07-03 |
| Arnon Yaari (`<email redacted>`) | 217 | 232 | 13.6% | 2020-01-25 |
| Nathaniel J. Smith | 169 | 196 | 10.6% | 2022-10-16 |

Of 1,597 attributed non-blank Python lines, these three hold 77%. Another 44 authors hold about 6%.

**Exposure**

- **Hartley:** Bus factor is 1. He alone holds 52.8% of the Python lines, and he is the only active author with a large share. Four authors committed in the last year, and their combined share is 55.9%. Losing him leaves almost no one who has changed the code recently. Capturing his knowledge while he is still reachable is the most important step before the handover.
- **Arnon and Nathaniel:** Together they hold 386 lines, 24.2% of the Python code. Neither has committed in years, so the tool counts their lines as having no active owner. Whether they still answer questions is a separate matter that git history cannot show. Their questions should be asked now.
- **Overall:** The active authors hold 892 lines (55.9%). The other 44.1% belongs to people with no commit in the last year.

**Caveats**

- **Survival, not understanding:** These numbers measure which lines survive and who last changed them. They say nothing about who understands the design, who reviewed the work, or what was deleted on purpose.
- **Reformat commit:** Commit c73f380, "Apply black-style string quotes", is set aside by default, so its lines go back to earlier authors. If you count it instead, Hartley's Python share falls to 713 lines (44.6%) of the 1,597. The tool then reports a bus factor of 2, because Hartley and Dana Kessler together lead the plain count. Dana Kessler holds 14 non-blank lines (0.5%) across the whole repository, from the brief. I did not print her Python-only figure.
- **Merges:** About a quarter of commits are merges. Their lines are credited to the authors of the merged branch commits, not to whoever merged.
- **Arnon's identity:** This is a proposal, not a confirmed fact. The tool suggests that `<email redacted>` and `<email redacted>` are one person, based only on names and addresses. I have not applied the merge and have not confirmed it. Unmerged, the `<email redacted>` address holds 217 Python lines (13.6%) in my Python-only run. The `<email redacted>` address holds 2 lines in the whole-repository brief, and its Python-only figure was not printed. Across the whole repository, the unmerged figures are 359 lines for `<email redacted>` and 2 for `<email redacted>`. If they are one person, the proposed merged total is 361 lines (13.7%).
- **Dates:** The tool's activity window is anchored to 2026-09-12, not today (2026-10-24). This is unlikely to change the conclusions, since the last-commit dates are well outside any window that matters.
- **Scope:** The whole-repository brief gives Hartley 55.6%. It includes non-Python files such as `pyproject.toml` and the README, which I did not re-run separately.

